{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-216177",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "To get rid of fractions, we multiply the first equation by $4$ and the second equation by $3,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}x+2y=-6 & |\\times 4 \\\\ -x+\\frac{1}{3}y=-10 & |\\times 3\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}3x+8y=-24 \\\\ -3x+y=-30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the elimination method to solve the system. We can eliminate $x$ by adding the two equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{3x}+8y & =-24 \\\\ -\\cancel{3x}+y & =-30 \\\\ (8y+y) & =(-24-30) \\\\ 9y & =-54 \\\\ y & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=-6$ into the first equation to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+8y & =-24 \\\\ 3x+8(-6) & =-24 \\\\ 3x-48 & =-24 \\\\ 3x & =24 \\\\ x & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=8,\\,y=-6.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
